<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rainier III, Prince of Monaco</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1923">1923</a>–<a href="/years/2005">2005</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-grimaldi">House of Grimaldi</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rainier III was Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monaco">Monaco</a> from 1949 to 2005, the longest reign in its history. He enlarged the principality through land reclamation, developed it as a banking and tourism centre and granted the constitution of 1962. His marriage to the American actress Grace Kelly in 1956 brought Monaco worldwide attention. His son Albert II succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rainier-iii-prince-of-monaco.jpg" alt="Rainier III, Prince of Monaco" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert LeRoy Knudsen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rainier_III_(JFKWHP-KN-C17888,_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rainier Louis Henri Maxence Bertrand Grimaldi was born at the Prince's Palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monaco">Monaco</a> on 31 May 1923, the first native-born prince since Honoré IV in 1758. He was the only son of Hereditary Princess Charlotte, whom her father Louis II had legitimised by adoption, and Count Pierre of Polignac, who took the Grimaldi name on marriage. His parents divorced in 1933. He was educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> at Summerfields and Stowe School, then at the Institut Le Rosey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, the University of Montpellier and Sciences Po in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. In 1944 his mother renounced her right to the throne and he became Louis II's direct heir. He joined the Free French Army that September and received the Croix de Guerre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Rainier became Sovereign Prince at 25, when Louis II died on 9 May 1949. He found a treasury that was practically empty, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monaco">Monaco</a>'s traditional gambling clientele had less money after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and rival gambling centres had opened. He chose to promote Monaco as a tax haven, a commercial centre and a tourist attraction. The Greek shipping owner Aristotle Onassis took control of the Société des Bains de Mer and wanted a gambling resort alone, but Rainier regained control in 1964.</p><p>He suspended the constitution in 1959 and ratified a new one in 1962. It reduced the sovereign's power, ended autocratic rule and placed power with both the prince and a National Council of eighteen elected members. In 1956 he married the American film actress Grace Kelly, with a civil ceremony on 18 April and a religious wedding at Saint Nicholas Cathedral on 19 April, watched by more than 30 million people. Their children were Caroline, Albert and Stéphanie.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Princess Grace died in 1982 after a car crash caused by a cerebral haemorrhage, and Rainier did not remarry. He founded the Princess Grace Foundation-USA that year to support young American artists, and established a postal museum in 1995. He died of complications of a lung infection on 6 April 2005, aged 81, and was buried beside his wife. His son succeeded him as Albert II. His reign lasted 56 years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rainier III of Monaco” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
